1
00:00:03,595 --> 00:00:08,816
[SPEAKER_00]: This is Sāla Suttang, Samyutta Nikaya 36.6.

2
00:00:12,577 --> 00:00:22,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Because an uninstructed worldling, a patujana, feels a pleasant feeling, feels a painful feeling, and feels a neither painful nor pleasant feeling.

3
00:00:23,963 --> 00:00:34,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Because an instructed noble disciple, an ārya, feels a pleasant feeling, feels a painful feeling, and feels a neither painful nor pleasant feeling.

4
00:00:35,728 --> 00:00:37,530
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because what is the distinction then?

5
00:00:37,850 --> 00:00:38,912
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the disparity?

6
00:00:39,272 --> 00:00:43,396
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the difference between the ārya and the putujana?

7
00:00:43,657 --> 00:00:46,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Between a noble disciple and a warling?

8
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:50,925
[SPEAKER_00]: Because both feel the three types of feeling.

9
00:00:51,245 --> 00:00:53,608
[SPEAKER_00]: But what is the difference between those?

10
00:00:55,370 --> 00:00:56,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Different individuals.

11
00:00:57,032 --> 00:01:01,856
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, the monk said, Bhante, I'm teaching the roots in the Buddha, so, please, please explain.

12
00:01:03,157 --> 00:01:04,278
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, the Buddha continues.

13
00:01:04,838 --> 00:01:19,110
[SPEAKER_00]: Because an uninstructed ordinary person, being touched by a painful feeling, he sorrows, he's troubled, he laments, beats his breast, cries, becomes bewildered.

14
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:23,233
[SPEAKER_00]: He feels two feelings, bodily and mental.

15
00:01:25,093 --> 00:01:30,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Suppose, because a man was shot by an arrow, he would be shot in the wound by a second arrow.

16
00:01:31,338 --> 00:01:34,341
[SPEAKER_00]: In this way, because the man feels the feeling of two arrows.

17
00:01:35,102 --> 00:01:44,670
[SPEAKER_00]: In just this way, an uninstructed worldly ordinary person, being touched by painful feelings, sorrows, his trouble, laments, beats his breast, cries, becomes confused.

18
00:01:44,830 --> 00:01:46,652
[SPEAKER_00]: He feels two feelings bodily and mental.

19
00:01:47,925 --> 00:01:56,792
[SPEAKER_00]: So here, the point that's quite important is the distinguishing between bodily and mental feeling.

20
00:01:57,553 --> 00:02:04,458
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a common assumption that a bodily feeling is basically what people regard as bodily sensations.

21
00:02:05,299 --> 00:02:05,639
[SPEAKER_00]: And so on.

22
00:02:05,659 --> 00:02:09,901
[SPEAKER_00]: Like things you perceive in your body, that are uncomfortable and so on.

23
00:02:10,622 --> 00:02:12,963
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's completely not the case.

24
00:02:13,583 --> 00:02:16,385
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said himself, every feeling is mental.

25
00:02:16,845 --> 00:02:17,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Cetasika.

26
00:02:17,725 --> 00:02:22,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Every feeling is basically felt on the level of the mind.

27
00:02:23,008 --> 00:02:27,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is then... how then should be... should we understand the bodily feeling?

28
00:02:28,431 --> 00:02:42,338
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it says... it says quite clearly bodily feeling is anything... any feeling that you experience mentally on account of things that you can perceive with your body or within your body.

29
00:02:42,918 --> 00:02:46,340
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, anything on account of your sixth sense base.

30
00:02:47,876 --> 00:02:53,938
[SPEAKER_00]: So, literally, any feeling that you have on account of a sight, is a feeling arisen on the bodily level.

31
00:02:54,738 --> 00:03:07,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, if you start grieving further, on account of that sight, and lamenting, and falling into despair, that's now the second feeling that's further mental.

32
00:03:08,542 --> 00:03:14,104
[SPEAKER_00]: So, bodily and mental are both mental, in the sense of both are felt with the mind,

33
00:03:14,842 --> 00:03:17,543
[SPEAKER_00]: But difference is where they came from, so to speak.

34
00:03:18,584 --> 00:03:29,328
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any bodily feeling is any discomfort you perceive by the body, or any comfort that you perceive by the body, internally or externally, or both.

35
00:03:30,068 --> 00:03:36,411
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not just for the internal things you perceive in the body, nor is it just for the external things that you perceive with the body.

36
00:03:37,022 --> 00:03:37,422
[SPEAKER_00]: It's both.

37
00:03:38,143 --> 00:03:55,716
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have, if you have, if you're experiencing feelings on account of any of the sense, any of the sense objects, whether it's sight, sound, smell, taste, touch, anything you've seen, heard and so on, if there is a feeling arisen on that account, that is a bodily feeling.

38
00:03:57,858 --> 00:04:05,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, both puthujjana and anārya would be experiencing that type of bodily feeling.

39
00:04:06,564 --> 00:04:20,469
[SPEAKER_00]: And for that matter, in a way, but we'll see that later, arahant also experience, well, bodily feeling, but you wouldn't call it a feeling if you choose the word feeling to be applicable to the feeling that you know as puthujjana.

40
00:04:20,969 --> 00:04:25,510
[SPEAKER_00]: So the complete absence of puthujjana's feeling, then you can understand what arahant's feeling is.

41
00:04:25,570 --> 00:04:27,251
[SPEAKER_00]: But as I said, that's maybe a different topic.

42
00:04:27,951 --> 00:04:30,212
[SPEAKER_00]: So now, the difference between an ārya

43
00:04:31,143 --> 00:04:43,956
[SPEAKER_00]: And between a pūtugina, the worldly ordinary uninstructed person, is they both experience these feelings on account of their senses, internally, externally, internally and externally.

44
00:04:44,617 --> 00:04:54,106
[SPEAKER_00]: But the difference is, pūtugina mentally has no escape from that feeling, which is why he will always have

45
00:04:54,887 --> 00:04:57,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Pretty much a second feeling on account of it.

46
00:04:57,548 --> 00:04:58,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Simultaneously present there.

47
00:04:59,409 --> 00:05:09,312
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on account of the discomfort, the pain touched through... touching you through the senses, something you've seen, heard, taste that you don't like, that frightens you and so on.

48
00:05:09,952 --> 00:05:14,714
[SPEAKER_00]: On account of that discomfort, you will be suffering mentally, simultaneously with it.

49
00:05:15,895 --> 00:05:16,615
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what it means.

50
00:05:16,895 --> 00:05:22,537
[SPEAKER_00]: You're hit by one arrow and immediately, basically, the second arrow would shoot you in the wound.

51
00:05:25,363 --> 00:05:32,167
[SPEAKER_00]: So, furthermore, an uninstructed worldly person, being touched by painful feeling, he will feel repulsion.

52
00:05:32,967 --> 00:05:38,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Repelled by this painful feeling, the underlying tendency to repulsion lies behind this.

53
00:05:39,107 --> 00:05:42,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Being touched by painful feeling, he delights in the pleasure of sense desires.

54
00:05:42,631 --> 00:05:43,411
[SPEAKER_00]: For what reason?

55
00:05:44,032 --> 00:05:51,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Because bhikkhus, the uninstructed worldling, does not understand any other escape from painful feeling other than the pleasure of sense desires.

56
00:05:52,040 --> 00:05:53,642
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it just continues.

57
00:05:53,822 --> 00:05:56,985
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having experienced something that pressures you through your senses,

58
00:05:58,279 --> 00:05:59,680
[SPEAKER_00]: You're experiencing discomfort.

59
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:04,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Not knowing the escape from that discomfort, you start experiencing mental anguish on account of it.

60
00:06:04,621 --> 00:06:20,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Not knowing the escape from that mental anguish, you then... the only thing you know is turn back to the same senses that caused you that mental anguish, and just look for things that are pleasant in them, and try to replace the displeasing things with pleasing things.

61
00:06:20,966 --> 00:06:24,327
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that's definition of sensuality and sensual craving.

62
00:06:25,107 --> 00:06:33,151
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's... this is the further... this passage, furthermore, just... just basically sheds more light on how that bodily feeling should be understood.

63
00:06:33,211 --> 00:06:39,114
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, it's not random sensations or things you perceive on account of a sense of touch of your body.

64
00:06:39,654 --> 00:06:44,997
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically feelings you're experiencing on account of things that you perceive through your senses.

65
00:06:46,017 --> 00:06:53,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why, exactly, by not knowing escape from that, you would turn back to the same sense objects, just trying to replace them with... with those that are agreeable.

66
00:06:55,402 --> 00:07:12,469
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh... because the eh... because... because the uninstructed ordinary person does not understand escape from painful feeling, other than the pleasure of sense desires, that's why for him, delighting in the pleasure of sense desires, then the underlying tendency of passion towards pleasant feeling lies behind this.

67
00:07:13,689 --> 00:07:18,891
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't understand, as it is, the origin, the gratification, the danger and the escape of those feelings.

68
00:07:20,151 --> 00:07:22,934
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if he feels a pleasant feeling, he feels connected to it, as if he's bound by it.

69
00:07:37,609 --> 00:07:40,211
[SPEAKER_00]: If he feels a painful feeling, he's bound by it.

70
00:07:40,692 --> 00:07:45,135
[SPEAKER_00]: If he feels neither painful nor pleasant feeling, he feels bound by it.

71
00:07:46,076 --> 00:07:58,346
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this bhikkhu is called an uninstructed, only person who is connected, who is bound by birth, aging, death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, grief, despair, unhappiness, trouble, who is bound with suffering, I say.

72
00:08:00,148 --> 00:08:02,910
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just to just to recap,

73
00:08:04,693 --> 00:08:13,857
[SPEAKER_00]: A puṭṭhujana feels the feelings that have arisen on account of his body, on account of things... and when you say body, you mean six-sense base.

74
00:08:14,177 --> 00:08:14,977
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what body is.

75
00:08:16,478 --> 00:08:25,481
[SPEAKER_00]: You know the body because of the six senses, and you know the six senses because there is a unit called body, that they basically consist of.

76
00:08:27,102 --> 00:08:32,984
[SPEAKER_00]: So, puṭṭhujana experiences a feeling on account of

77
00:08:34,815 --> 00:09:03,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything that can come through his senses, whether it's a sense of touch, like uncomfortable bodily sensations, like, I don't know, rough stone you have to sleep on, heat, cold, or even if it's something like a displeasing sight, frightening sound, a disagreeable disgusting taste, horrible smell, any of these things that you would be perceiving there on the level of perception,

78
00:09:04,458 --> 00:09:10,283
[SPEAKER_00]: They do cause you, simultaneously, to be pressured by an unpleasant feeling in regard to it.

79
00:09:10,703 --> 00:09:11,504
[SPEAKER_00]: It's disagreeable.

80
00:09:12,144 --> 00:09:17,409
[SPEAKER_00]: You feel it unpleasantly.

81
00:09:17,449 --> 00:09:21,012
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is absolutely no problem with this, if, then,

82
00:09:22,208 --> 00:09:23,830
[SPEAKER_00]: The patujana would know the escape of it.

83
00:09:23,910 --> 00:09:48,092
[SPEAKER_00]: If he would understand the impermanence of the sense bases, the impossibility, the inherent lack of ownership of your own body, like fully understood that, not just occasionally thought about it, it would be impossible for him, well, he wouldn't be a patujana, but it would be impossible for him to be pressured by any feeling that would arise in regard to that which has been understood as completely impermanent.

84
00:09:49,315 --> 00:09:53,656
[SPEAKER_00]: impossible to own, cannot pertain to you, cannot bother you, in that sense.

85
00:09:54,276 --> 00:09:58,497
[SPEAKER_00]: But, by not understanding that, he gets hit by the second arrow of pain.

86
00:09:58,557 --> 00:10:06,538
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not just that you're pressured, but then you recognize that you have absolutely no means of escape from that pressure of that painful feeling.

87
00:10:06,558 --> 00:10:08,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, literally, you can't shut down your...

88
00:10:08,998 --> 00:10:13,639
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can pull your eyes out, and still, you can suffer an account of absence of vision now.

89
00:10:13,759 --> 00:10:14,059
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever.

90
00:10:14,319 --> 00:10:15,519
[SPEAKER_00]: The visual domain remains.

91
00:10:15,599 --> 00:10:18,880
[SPEAKER_00]: The senses remain intact, even if you start trying to destroy them.

92
00:10:20,075 --> 00:10:28,461
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why when those people back in the day said to the Buddha, oh, they practice by not seeing things and not hearing things with their ears and not seeing things with their eyes.

93
00:10:28,501 --> 00:10:31,183
[SPEAKER_00]: And he says, you're no different than a blind and a deaf man.

94
00:10:32,083 --> 00:10:33,164
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no wisdom in that.

95
00:10:33,184 --> 00:10:35,446
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just avoiding because you don't know where the problem is.

96
00:10:36,745 --> 00:10:44,532
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not knowing where the problem is, you don't know the escape from the problem, you then suffer twice as much, so to speak.

97
00:10:44,772 --> 00:10:49,937
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is suffering on account of simple disagreeability of certain sense experience.

98
00:10:50,778 --> 00:10:52,780
[SPEAKER_00]: So, bodily suffering, you can call it.

99
00:10:53,581 --> 00:10:57,544
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, because not knowing the escape, there is the mental suffering involved.

100
00:10:57,604 --> 00:10:58,525
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, think of it like this.

101
00:10:58,785 --> 00:11:01,328
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's something bothering you, physically, say.

102
00:11:02,850 --> 00:11:09,136
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is also a possibility, a certain prospect within that thing, that it might be life-threatening.

103
00:11:09,897 --> 00:11:14,401
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in itself, it's not impossible to endure that certain discomfort.

104
00:11:15,122 --> 00:11:23,810
[SPEAKER_00]: You could endure it, but there is a thought attached to it, that this actually might be causing the ending of your life, might kill you.

105
00:11:24,727 --> 00:11:31,695
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the significance, even of that which you could bear, is now far worse, because you don't know the escape from it.

106
00:11:31,755 --> 00:11:34,639
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know how to prevent it, and your life is at stake.

107
00:11:36,114 --> 00:11:37,815
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the real suffering here.

108
00:11:37,855 --> 00:11:41,899
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that discomfort on account of body, bodily feeling that you're experiencing.

109
00:11:42,379 --> 00:11:50,205
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that mental anguish on account of not knowing the escape and recognizing that, well, if it doesn't crush you now, it will at some point in the future.

110
00:11:50,245 --> 00:11:59,773
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Buddha said, by being bound to those feelings that have arisen on account of sense organs of your body, you are bound by sickness, aging, death, despair.

111
00:11:59,813 --> 00:12:00,914
[SPEAKER_00]: You're bound by saṃsara.

112
00:12:00,934 --> 00:12:02,075
[SPEAKER_00]: You're bound by the whole thing.

113
00:12:06,962 --> 00:12:08,443
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the real problem there.

114
00:12:08,603 --> 00:12:10,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Not knowing the escape is where the dukkha is.

115
00:12:11,586 --> 00:12:17,571
[SPEAKER_00]: And which is why, exactly for Sotāpanna, he becomes a Sotāpanna, he becomes an Ārya by knowing the escape from dukkha.

116
00:12:17,591 --> 00:12:21,835
[SPEAKER_00]: The Four Noble Truths is a definition of escape from the dukkha that has been understood.

117
00:12:24,697 --> 00:12:31,223
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, the same Putujana, who now has this mental anguish, and by the way, even the the agreeable

118
00:12:32,372 --> 00:12:35,254
[SPEAKER_00]: The pleasure that touches your body, it still pressures you.

119
00:12:35,274 --> 00:12:38,536
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still felt on the level of that bodily feeling.

120
00:12:38,656 --> 00:12:39,717
[SPEAKER_00]: It's felt unpleasantly.

121
00:12:39,737 --> 00:12:41,718
[SPEAKER_00]: We covered that in other talks.

122
00:12:41,878 --> 00:12:45,300
[SPEAKER_00]: You can, I think, if you look at the... what is it?

123
00:12:46,521 --> 00:12:49,963
[SPEAKER_00]: The sensual... overcoming sensuality playlist.

124
00:12:50,143 --> 00:12:50,704
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all there.

125
00:12:51,164 --> 00:12:58,769
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is that even the seemingly agreeable prospect of of sensual gratification, it's first felt unpleasantly.

126
00:12:58,849 --> 00:13:00,070
[SPEAKER_00]: And this sutta repeats that.

127
00:13:02,126 --> 00:13:08,769
[SPEAKER_00]: So, anything felt through the body pressures you, bothers you, so to speak.

128
00:13:09,089 --> 00:13:11,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the simile of the flayed cow.

129
00:13:11,691 --> 00:13:15,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Whichever way you lean or experience, it's going to be bothersome.

130
00:13:16,253 --> 00:13:19,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Not knowing the escape, you get the mental anguish on account of it.

131
00:13:19,775 --> 00:13:27,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Not knowing the escape from that, you then turn back the only direction you know, which is the direction of the senses that cause the whole thing.

132
00:13:27,598 --> 00:13:30,520
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the irony of the whole central craving.

133
00:13:31,303 --> 00:13:37,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sensuality is painful, you seek it, in order to overcome that pain.

134
00:13:37,845 --> 00:13:42,246
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to basically replace that pain, you seek that which causes the pain.

135
00:13:43,426 --> 00:13:46,347
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha compared sensual attachment to madness.

136
00:13:46,627 --> 00:13:50,988
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, the Upatūjanas technically are mad, because it's a level of madness.

137
00:13:51,128 --> 00:13:55,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Or gave the other similes, like burning yourself with the burning coal.

138
00:13:57,105 --> 00:14:06,896
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the embers burning your skin, because it offers you some sort of numbing gratification of your sort of itchy disease and so on.

139
00:14:07,877 --> 00:14:11,661
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not knowing the escape from that discomfort, you turn back

140
00:14:12,860 --> 00:14:19,782
[SPEAKER_00]: To the very thing that caused you that discomfort, and then just try to seek less uncomfortable things, which is what sensuality is.

141
00:14:20,502 --> 00:14:22,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality is not actually pleasant.

142
00:14:22,823 --> 00:14:27,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality is simply less painful from other more painful things.

143
00:14:27,484 --> 00:14:37,647
[SPEAKER_00]: And less pain from the perspective of pain, especially if you want to get rid of the pain, is going to be seen as pleasant, as extremely wanted and welcomed.

144
00:14:42,343 --> 00:14:47,928
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what Buddha means by by patujana, not understanding any escape from painful feeling, any other escape.

145
00:14:48,917 --> 00:14:52,619
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the pleasure of the sense desires, he goes for the pleasure of sense desires.

146
00:14:52,639 --> 00:14:54,080
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why he cultivates the delight.

147
00:14:54,160 --> 00:15:05,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, next time anything uncomfortable touches him, he simultaneously... his mind is in a state of a context, basically, that values escape from that discomfort through sensuality.

148
00:15:05,445 --> 00:15:09,888
[SPEAKER_00]: So, simply by experiencing this comfort, he already delights in comfort.

149
00:15:10,248 --> 00:15:14,530
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if he's not necessarily seeking it, the mind will hold the value of comfort.

150
00:15:14,590 --> 00:15:15,911
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he'll be delighting in it.

151
00:15:16,910 --> 00:15:19,291
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what the Buddha refers to these underlying tendencies.

152
00:15:19,771 --> 00:15:23,792
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to stop delighting in comfort and stop delighting in trying to get rid of the pain.

153
00:15:27,173 --> 00:15:33,715
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then he goes on, the sutta carries on, then, eh... about the Ārya-sāukha, the instructed noble disciple.

154
00:15:34,655 --> 00:15:37,436
[SPEAKER_00]: When he's touched by painful feeling, he does not sorrow.

155
00:15:38,216 --> 00:15:39,997
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not troubled, he doesn't lament.

156
00:15:40,117 --> 00:15:42,017
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now, why wouldn't he not sorrow?

157
00:15:42,217 --> 00:15:42,937
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the difference?

158
00:15:42,957 --> 00:15:44,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the difference is he understood

159
00:15:46,140 --> 00:15:54,006
[SPEAKER_00]: The implicit, the thoroughly understood, the implicit impermanence of that on account of which feelings have arisen.

160
00:15:54,186 --> 00:15:55,266
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's important to say.

161
00:15:55,326 --> 00:15:57,688
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't say, oh, he understood feelings are impermanent.

162
00:15:59,509 --> 00:16:01,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he understood that too, by proxy.

163
00:16:02,051 --> 00:16:07,114
[SPEAKER_00]: But you need to understand, as the Buddha would say, well, that because of which feelings are.

164
00:16:07,374 --> 00:16:08,535
[SPEAKER_00]: The origin of feelings.

165
00:16:09,807 --> 00:16:15,892
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to understand that that already is impermanent, let alone then the feeling that has arisen on account of it.

166
00:16:15,912 --> 00:16:23,799
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not just a question of repeating to yourself that whatever you're currently feeling, oh, this is impermanent, it's not mine, it's not mine, I can't own it, there is no sense of control.

167
00:16:24,280 --> 00:16:27,583
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just management on account of you already being bothered by the feeling.

168
00:16:28,490 --> 00:16:33,073
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to see through that presently enduring feeling, see what it depends on.

169
00:16:33,474 --> 00:16:34,435
[SPEAKER_00]: So you realize, whatever.

170
00:16:34,955 --> 00:16:41,500
[SPEAKER_00]: Take a pleasant or unpleasant feeling, say in this case, unpleasant feeling, on account of... so you're touched by something painful.

171
00:16:42,851 --> 00:16:53,514
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mentally, you're touched by pain on account of, I don't know, seeing... seeing somebody you really dislike, somebody that hurt you in the past, or something like that, somebody you had ill will towards.

172
00:16:54,254 --> 00:16:58,555
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just the mere sight of that person on the street and you're experiencing mental pain.

173
00:16:59,995 --> 00:17:04,236
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't want to now just be trying to get rid of that pain or explain to yourself that that pain is impermanent.

174
00:17:04,776 --> 00:17:06,697
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to see that your eyes are impermanent.

175
00:17:06,837 --> 00:17:08,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, would you be able

176
00:17:09,865 --> 00:17:13,986
[SPEAKER_00]: To experience that feeling, if there were no sight whatsoever...

177
00:17:17,247 --> 00:17:17,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

178
00:17:18,107 --> 00:17:21,268
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't a kind of sight and it requires your memory of the same sight.

179
00:17:21,728 --> 00:17:22,408
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible.

180
00:17:22,508 --> 00:17:24,069
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still within the sight domain.

181
00:17:25,509 --> 00:17:30,070
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, are you in a position to choose what sights will arise for you?

182
00:17:30,370 --> 00:17:31,891
[SPEAKER_00]: What sights will not arise for you?

183
00:17:32,231 --> 00:17:33,111
[SPEAKER_00]: What sounds will arise?

184
00:17:33,671 --> 00:17:37,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes you can exercise some sense of control, but overall, which is more?

185
00:17:38,684 --> 00:17:43,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes you've seen things you couldn't see, you didn't want to see, or times you've seen things you want to see.

186
00:17:43,547 --> 00:17:47,109
[SPEAKER_00]: It's far more things you don't want to see, things you didn't plan of seeing.

187
00:17:47,569 --> 00:17:53,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe you decided you want to see them later, but the point is, you have no say in your eyes seeing or not seeing.

188
00:17:53,672 --> 00:17:54,433
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not up to you.

189
00:17:55,833 --> 00:18:03,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Not dwelling sufficiently enough of that recognition, that's how you basically start appropriating your eyes and your sights.

190
00:18:03,978 --> 00:18:07,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why then you get pressure on account of feelings that arise on account of sights.

191
00:18:09,433 --> 00:18:16,317
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't forget that your own very eyes are not... cannot be yours, even if you want them to.

192
00:18:16,697 --> 00:18:21,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if they could be yours, they wouldn't age, they wouldn't be liable to sickness, accidents and so on.

193
00:18:21,139 --> 00:18:22,180
[SPEAKER_00]: They would never stop working.

194
00:18:23,260 --> 00:18:24,561
[SPEAKER_00]: But they're clearly not yours.

195
00:18:24,681 --> 00:18:36,347
[SPEAKER_00]: So they just keep providing you with this content of sights, and same goes for any other organ, and you just keep taking it up all for granted, as if you created it, as if it's there because of you.

196
00:18:40,233 --> 00:18:51,822
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the impermanence that needs to be... that's the... that's the level of anicca, saṅkhara anicca, not dhamma anicca, because when you are concerned about your feeling, thinking about your present feeling, at that moment, that is the dhamma of your experience.

197
00:18:52,723 --> 00:19:00,509
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to see the saṅkhara of that dhamma, as other suttas say, sabhe saṅkhara anicca, sabhe saṅkhara dukkha, sabhe dhamma anatta.

198
00:19:00,549 --> 00:19:06,053
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this feeling is going to be seen as not-self, when you see that that upon which that feeling

199
00:19:10,153 --> 00:19:11,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Not pleasant, not worth owning.

200
00:19:12,455 --> 00:19:15,197
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what an instructed noble disciple has understood.

201
00:19:16,578 --> 00:19:24,345
[SPEAKER_00]: He has understood, in this particular example, the extent of the body and any feeling that can arise on account of the body.

202
00:19:24,645 --> 00:19:27,908
[SPEAKER_00]: And when I say the extent, I mean the extent, the nature of it, the characteristics.

203
00:19:27,968 --> 00:19:28,808
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot be owned.

204
00:19:29,209 --> 00:19:36,595
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it becomes inconceivable for him to grieve on account of that which cannot... it's just... it's impossible to even imagine the ownership of it.

205
00:19:38,641 --> 00:19:49,251
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason people still keep imagining the ownership of their senses, is because they never make sufficient amount of effort to think through correctly, that it cannot be owned.

206
00:19:51,013 --> 00:19:54,996
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, any effort that's made, it's usually made then on the level of the feeling.

207
00:19:55,877 --> 00:19:59,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Our feeling is not mine, feeling is anicca, feeling is anicca, when feeling bothers you.

208
00:20:00,382 --> 00:20:01,543
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not the right level.

209
00:20:01,983 --> 00:20:03,545
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to see through that feeling,

210
00:20:04,463 --> 00:20:06,285
[SPEAKER_00]: That which... because of which the feeling is.

211
00:20:07,066 --> 00:20:09,128
[SPEAKER_00]: So, understanding that saṅkharas are impermanent.

212
00:20:09,368 --> 00:20:10,829
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... that's what that statement means.

213
00:20:11,950 --> 00:20:22,241
[SPEAKER_00]: So, bhikkhus and ārya, being attached by painful feeling, he doesn't sorrow, he's not troubled, does not lament, does not beat... beat his breast, does not cry and does not become confused.

214
00:20:23,182 --> 00:20:26,565
[SPEAKER_00]: He feels only one feeling on account of the body, not mental.

215
00:20:28,355 --> 00:20:30,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Suppose Bhikkhu's a man was shot by an arrow.

216
00:20:30,357 --> 00:20:33,179
[SPEAKER_00]: He would be... he would not be shot in the wound by a second arrow.

217
00:20:33,860 --> 00:20:36,822
[SPEAKER_00]: In this way, Bhikkhu's the man feels the feeling of one arrow.

218
00:20:37,483 --> 00:20:47,832
[SPEAKER_00]: In just this way, Bhikkhu's an uninstructed noble disciple, being touched by painful feeling, does not sorrow, he's not troubled, does not lament, does not beat his breast, does not cry and does not become confused.

219
00:20:48,032 --> 00:20:51,335
[SPEAKER_00]: He feels one feeling, on account of body, not on account of his mind.

220
00:20:53,653 --> 00:20:58,157
[SPEAKER_00]: And for him, being touched by painful feeling, there is no repulsion.

221
00:20:59,378 --> 00:21:04,502
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is no aversion towards that painful feeling, which is any feeling at that level.

222
00:21:05,463 --> 00:21:08,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Any feeling is pressuring you, thus any feeling is painful feeling.

223
00:21:09,726 --> 00:21:13,369
[SPEAKER_00]: But he's not averse to pain, because he doesn't need to be.

224
00:21:13,469 --> 00:21:18,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would he want to be rid of the pain, when he knows that it's not his concern?

225
00:21:19,454 --> 00:21:20,535
[SPEAKER_00]: He hasn't created it.

226
00:21:21,660 --> 00:21:27,282
[SPEAKER_00]: It has a reason on its own, and he has understood that whatever arises on its own, that's why it cannot... it has to pass.

227
00:21:27,682 --> 00:21:28,723
[SPEAKER_00]: It has to come to an end.

228
00:21:29,663 --> 00:21:30,723
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is nothing for him.

229
00:21:30,963 --> 00:21:38,146
[SPEAKER_00]: He cannot think, oh, this will last forever and bother me forever, which is usually that irrational thought of, oh, this this pain will be forever.

230
00:21:38,326 --> 00:21:39,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Even people know they won't.

231
00:21:40,067 --> 00:21:41,087
[SPEAKER_00]: It won't be there forever.

232
00:21:41,507 --> 00:21:45,729
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't help but keep thinking, it will be forever, so I must get rid of it.

233
00:21:45,849 --> 00:21:49,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Just this once, I must get rid of it, and then later on I'll contemplate that it won't be forever.

234
00:21:50,853 --> 00:21:56,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, an Ārya cannot think that anymore, because he's fully understood that whatever has the nature of arising, that's why has nature to cease.

235
00:21:58,238 --> 00:22:03,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when he's touched by painful feeling, he has no... well, it's impossible for him to be averse towards it.

236
00:22:04,863 --> 00:22:11,947
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not being averse towards this painful feeling, the underlying tendency towards aversion does not lie behind this.

237
00:22:13,969 --> 00:22:18,352
[SPEAKER_00]: So, being touched by painful feeling, he doesn't delight then in pleasure of sense desires.

238
00:22:19,322 --> 00:22:26,466
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if he's not, then, touched by that second pain, why would he then need to try to get rid of it when it's not there?

239
00:22:27,406 --> 00:22:31,408
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when it's not there, means the basis for wanting sense pleasures is not there.

240
00:22:33,529 --> 00:22:34,230
[SPEAKER_00]: For what reason?

241
00:22:34,410 --> 00:22:39,132
[SPEAKER_00]: The instructed noble disciple understands the escape from painful feeling other than the pleasure of sense desires.

242
00:22:39,933 --> 00:22:46,176
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for him not delighting in the pleasure of sense desires, the underlying tendency of passion towards pleasant feeling does not lie behind this.

243
00:22:47,132 --> 00:22:53,053
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands, as it is, the origin, the gratification, the danger and the escape of those feelings.

244
00:22:54,153 --> 00:22:59,975
[SPEAKER_00]: We covered in other talks what the origin is, what the gratification, what the danger and what the escape is.

245
00:23:00,255 --> 00:23:04,776
[SPEAKER_00]: You might search the channel.

246
00:23:06,096 --> 00:23:15,218
[SPEAKER_00]: For him, understanding as it is, the origin, the gratification, the danger and the escape from this feeling, the underlying tendency towards ignorance regarding neither painful nor pleasant feeling does not lie behind this.

247
00:23:18,073 --> 00:23:21,714
[SPEAKER_00]: If he feels pleasant feeling, he feels it un... unconnected to it.

248
00:23:22,574 --> 00:23:23,654
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he's not bound by it.

249
00:23:24,694 --> 00:23:26,935
[SPEAKER_00]: If he feels a painful feeling, he is not bound by it.

250
00:23:27,155 --> 00:23:30,195
[SPEAKER_00]: If he feels neither painful nor pleasant feeling, he is not bound by it.

251
00:23:31,175 --> 00:23:42,838
[SPEAKER_00]: This bhikkhus is called an uninstructed noble... an instructed, sorry, an instructed noble disciple who is unconnected, unbound by birth, aging, death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, unhappiness, trouble.

252
00:23:43,498 --> 00:23:45,218
[SPEAKER_00]: He's unbound by suffering.

253
00:23:46,599 --> 00:23:54,342
[SPEAKER_00]: This because is the distinction, this is the disparity, this is the difference between an uninstructed noble disciple and an uninstructed ordinary person.

254
00:23:56,123 --> 00:23:58,744
[SPEAKER_00]: One with understanding does not feel a feeling.

255
00:23:59,684 --> 00:24:02,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasant, painful, one who has learned much.

256
00:24:03,285 --> 00:24:07,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Between the wise, clever one and the ordinary person, this is the big difference.

257
00:24:08,369 --> 00:24:17,455
[SPEAKER_00]: For one who has recognized the dhamma, one who has learned much, seeing this world and the next, things that are wished for do not disturb the mind.

258
00:24:18,516 --> 00:24:21,998
[SPEAKER_00]: And things, basically, that are not wished for, do not repel the mind.

259
00:24:24,120 --> 00:24:28,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for him, welcoming or opposing are extinguished, brought to an end, do not exist.

260
00:24:31,672 --> 00:24:39,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Having understood the stainless, sorrowless place, the one who has gone beyond being, understands these things correctly.

